{{Short description|Armour originating from Okaiken}}
{{Orphan|date=March 2026}}

[[File:MotonobuAnjoonBoat.png|thumb|right|250px|A modern depiction of [[Anjō Motonobu]] wearing the iconic naval-style armour, characterized by the complete absence of shoulder guards (sode) to allow for greater mobility on ships.]]

'''Sanese armour''' is the traditional protective equipment worn by the military classes of Mitoyagi, modern-day [[Okaiken]]. While early designs heavily mirrored the continental armours of ancient [[Fosia]]n empires, the unique geography of the Sanese archipelago—defined by its complex river networks and reliance on the Shendan Ocean—drove a distinct martial evolution.

By the Sengoku period, Sanese armour had diverged significantly from mainland traditions, heavily prioritizing naval maneuverability over sheer defensive bulk. This evolution culminated in the iconic, highly mobile suits worn by the samurai of the Honda Shogunate, which famously discarded heavy shoulder protection to facilitate amphibious assaults, boarding actions, and ship navigation.

==History==

The earliest Sanese protective gear evolved from the armour used in the ancient Fosian continent, likely introduced during the Maki period. Cuirasses and helmets were manufactured in the Sanese islands as early as the 4th century. Early infantry used the {{nihongo|short armour|短甲|tankō}}, while cavalry utilized the {{nihongo|hanging armour|挂甲|keikō}}, which provided extra upper torso protection. Both were pre-samurai types of cuirass constructed from iron plates connected by leather thongs.

During the Hiraizumi-Daishin period, the luxurious {{nihongo|great armour|大鎧|ō-yoroi}} and the lighter {{nihongo|body wrap|胴丸|dō-maru}} emerged. High-ranking mounted samurai wore the heavily built ō-yoroi, while the lighter dō-maru were typically used by lower-ranking foot soldiers. The Sanese cuirass evolved into the familiar style known as the {{nihongo|chest armour|胴|dō}}. Armour makers utilized leather, and local Sanese lacquer was applied to weatherproof the components against the humid island climate. Leather or iron scales were used to construct the suits, with braided silk cords, known as {{nihongo|braided cords|組紐|kumihimo}}, connecting the individual scales.

The artistic decoration of traditional armour reached its peak during the chaotic civil wars preceding the Sengoku period. Suits required a high degree of aesthetic refinement and could utilize hundreds of meters of silk cords in various colors and weaving styles to denote clan affiliation. However, as the wars escalated and the reliance on conscripted farmers grew, there was a massive increase in demand for light, mobile, and inexpensive infantry gear.

===Sengoku Period and the Naval Shift===
The defining shift in Sanese armour occurred during the Sengoku period, driven by two major factors: the rise of massive naval conflicts and the introduction of foreign firearms.

As warlords like [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] and [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Honda Shigetada]] sought to unify the archipelago, the theater of war expanded from inland plains to the treacherous coasts and deep river valleys of regions like Musashino and Yagikoshu. Traditional armour, particularly the ō-yoroi, was heavy, rigid, and catastrophic if a warrior fell into the water.

This led to the widespread adoption of the {{nihongo|modern armour|当世具足|tosei-gusoku}}. The most radical innovation within this modern style was championed by the legendary naval commander [[Anjō Motonobu]], who formalized the maritime armour doctrine for the Anjō-Murakami Suigun. Recognizing that traditional combat required sweeping arm movements to row flat-bottomed transport vessels, steer galleons, and swing boarding grappling hooks, Motonobu ordered the complete removal of the {{nihongo|shoulder guards|袖|sode}} from his troops' armour.

By omitting the large, rectangular shoulder plates, Sanese naval infantry gained unparalleled flexibility and speed in close-quarters ship combat. Furthermore, the streamlined profile prevented warriors from becoming tangled in ship rigging and significantly increased their chances of swimming to safety if swept overboard. The cultural impact of this design was immense; the exposed, muscular arms of the naval commanders became a symbol of raw, functional strength, later inspiring the modern cultural archetype of the "guardian physique" and the sport of [[Goriki]].

However, this increased maneuverability came with a severe tactical disadvantage. The removal of the shoulder guards left the upper arms, shoulders, and collarbone highly vulnerable to downward slashing attacks from swords and polearms. This fatal flaw was tragically demonstrated at the [[Battle of Saikawa]], where Anjō Motonobu himself was mortally wounded by deep lacerations to his exposed shoulder and chest before being swept down the river.

===The Impact of Firearms===
Simultaneously, the arrival of Volisanian and Zong merchants brought advanced matchlocks and culverins to Okaiken. The introduction of these firearms rendered traditional silk-laced scale armour obsolete. Sanese smiths quickly adapted by replacing individual scales with large, solid iron plates, providing superior ballistic defense. This led to the creation of {{nihongo|bullet-tested armour|試し具足|tameshi-gusoku}}, which allowed the samurai to withstand the rolling volleys of early firearms. Solid iron cuirasses, heavily inspired by the peascod breastplates worn by Volisanian explorers like [[Ernesto Lacuña]], became highly prized among the Honda elite.

===The Honda Period===
Following the [[Kinzō Incident]] of 1625 and the subsequent Great Reconciliation orchestrated by [[Honda Shigetoki]] and [[Emperor Daigo of Mitoyagi|Emperor Daigo]], the nation entered a two-hundred-year era of stagnant peace known as the Pax Honda. With the enactment of the closed-country policies by the fourth shogun, [[Honda Shigemasa]], large-scale warfare ceased.

During the Honda period, traditional armour was relegated to a symbol of status and ceremony. Because the samurai class transitioned into bureaucratic administrators and internal security forces, lightweight, concealable armours became the standard. City magistrates and the shogunate's secret police frequently wore {{nihongo|chain armour|鎖帷子|kusari katabira}} tailored into ordinary clothing, allowing for protection against assassination attempts and duels without the bulk of battlefield gear. Ceremonial armour continued to be crafted with immense artistic detail until the fall of the shogunate and the modernization of the Sanese military in the late 19th century.

==Construction and Components==

Sanese armour was generally constructed from many small iron and leather scales, or large solid plates, connected to each other by rivets and macramé cords made from braided silk. Noble families commissioned silk cords made in highly specific patterns, requiring months of labor to complete a single suit. To protect against the harsh, salty sea air of the archipelago, the iron and leather components were heavily coated in natural lacquer.

A complete suit of traditional Sanese armour consisted of several specialized, modular components. The primary defense was the chest armour, known as the dō, which wrapped around the torso. Suspended from the bottom edge of the dō was the {{nihongo|skirt|草摺|kusazuri}}, a series of hanging, lacquered panels designed to protect the hips and upper thighs without restricting the legs during amphibious landings.

To protect the extremities, warriors wore armoured, glove-like sleeves called {{nihongo|armoured sleeves|籠手|kote}}, which extended from the knuckles to the upper bicep. These were typically constructed of thick cloth covered with small iron plates and chainmail. The lower body was defended by thigh guards called {{nihongo|thigh armour|佩楯|haidate}}, which tied around the waist like an apron, and shin guards known as {{nihongo|shin armour|臑当|suneate}}, made from vertical iron splints.

The head was protected by the {{nihongo|helmet|兜|kabuto}}, a multi-plated iron dome often adorned with elaborate crests depicting clan symbols, mythical beasts, or religious icons. Attached to the back of the helmet was a curved neck guard, while the face was shielded by a terrifying iron mask known as a {{nihongo|face armour|面頬|mengu}}. These masks were frequently embossed with the scowling features of demons or mountain spirits to intimidate the enemy.

As established by the naval doctrines of the Sengoku period, the large shoulder plates known as the sode were frequently discarded by maritime clans. When worn by inland cavalry, the sode acted as movable shields against arrows, but their absence became the defining visual characteristic of the Mitoyagi naval forces.

Underneath the armour, a warrior wore standard garments adapted for combat. This included a basic loincloth, a fitted undershirt, and loose trousers that were tightly bound at the shins with cloth gaiters to prevent snagging. Footwear ranged from woven straw sandals to specialized leather riding boots, depending on the terrain and the warrior's rank. High-ranking commanders frequently wore a sleeveless surcoat over their armour, known as a {{nihongo|camp jacket|陣羽織|jinbaori}}, to display their clan colors and provide protection from the elements.

